  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM EDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nA fairly quiet stretch of weather is expected to start the week\nas a departing upper-level disturbance continues to pull \nfarther into the Northeast. In its wake, expanding mid-level \nridging and accompanying surface high pressure settle across the\nGreat Lakes, providing a few days of quiet weather to begin the\nforecast period. The larger-scale pattern remains progressive \nwith the aformentioned ridge gradually shifting east as another \nwave and its associated cold front approach the region mid-week.\nBeyond that, guidance continues to favor a return to broader \ntroughing across the region through late week into the start of \nthe weekend before indications of a flatter, more zonal flow \nlooks to emerge toward the end of the period. \n\nClearing skies overnight combined with light winds and \nlingering low- level moisture has allowed areas of patchy fog to\ndevelop this morning, especially near inland lakes, rivers, and\nother favored low- lying locations. After morning fog \ndissipates, abundant sunshine and dry conditions are expected \nboth today and Tuesday with today's afternoon high temperatures \ngenerally in the 70s to around 80 degrees. Overnight lows \ntonight will dip close tot he 40s across the typical interior \ncold spots, with the remainder of northern Michigan settling \ninto the mid 50s. As the surface high pressure shifts east, \ndeveloping southerly return flow and continued sunshine should \nsupport a modest warmup Tuesday, with many locations climbing \ninto the low to mid 80s. \n\nForecast confidence begins to decrease somewhat by Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday as the next aforementioned shortwave and \nattendant front move toward the area. Increasing large-scale \nascent associated with favorable upper-level jet dynamics will \nsupport a growing chance for showers and thunderstorms, with the\ngreatest coverage likely occurring sometime Wednesday as the \nfront crosses the region. Exact timing remains the primary \nforecast challenge and will ultimately dictate how much \ninstability can develop ahead of the boundary. Current \nindications suggest enough instability for scattered \nthunderstorms, with the potential for a few stronger storms \nshould greater daytime destabilization materializes. Moisture \nalso increases ahead of the front, supporting locally efficient \nrainfall rates where thunderstorms persist. \n\nConditions settle back down heading into the latter half of the\nweek as cooler air filters in behind the front beneath broader \ncyclonic flow aloft. While the overall pattern does not appear \nparticularly active, weak disturbances rotating through the \nupper-level trough may be sufficient enough to generate low-end \nshower chances from time to time. Seasonable temperatures look \nto persist through the end of the week.",
